      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "likeCount": 0,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "59f22f9333c3885a46fad120",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "value": "As you might imagine comprehensive moderation systems is something we have been thinking about since launch and ongoing. In fact, the very next patch will include some helpful features around moderation. Partially spurred by the community asking but also because we know it's important. Deputizing community members globally is not ideal. At least not in the short term. That would likely remain something we do as a team. For quite a few reasons. However, systems should exist to enable world authors to self moderate alone or with those they choose to deputize. Another set of systems that will help is a more granular social graph including groups, favorites and stats. Tracked user behavior (stats) should also help define how trusted a user is globally. The systems of which would not be fully disclosed much like the real world or online systems such as that used for twitter verification. That is done by those entities to slow the \"gaming\" of those systems. In simple terms though mods, world authors and ultimately everyone would have some of those stats bubbled up to them in a dashboard view. For example: If someone has 1 friend and has been blocked 20 times in a day and warned by a mod chances are they are trouble vs someone who's made 20 friends, uploaded a world with lots of visits and that we've met and talked to in VRChat. There's a system there that can be developed that may not be perfect but it becomes a good metric on which to evaluate user behavior. Much more than zero metrics anyway. We understand how special a place VRChat is and what is needed to both protect it but also afford users the freedom to have fun. It's key in my mind and that of the team. Thanks for the feedback.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
